WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.305
This is a working guide to the Android A2UI libraries as they stand in alpha.

2
00:00:08.305 --> 00:00:11.212
By the end you will know what is published and where

3
00:00:11.212 --> 00:00:15.517
how to wire a renderer into a Compose app, how the protocol works on the wire

4
00:00:15.517 --> 00:00:19.263
how data binding and user actions close the loop back to your agent

5
00:00:19.263 --> 00:00:22.953
and the ten traps that will otherwise each cost you a build cycle.

6
00:00:22.953 --> 00:00:25.972
Everything shown here is running code, not pseudocode.

7
00:00:25.972 --> 00:00:31.116
Two projects back it: one that renders a static payload, and one that talks to a live agent.

8
00:00:31.116 --> 00:00:33.352
Start with what is actually published.

9
00:00:33.352 --> 00:00:36.766
Five artifacts, spread across three separate Maven groups.

10
00:00:36.766 --> 00:00:40.415
The androidx dot a 2 u i group holds the data layer: the model

11
00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:43.182
which is schemas and data types, and the engine

12
00:00:43.182 --> 00:00:46.713
which parses protocol messages and drives the state machine.

13
00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:50.598
The androidx dot a 2 u i dot compose group is the renderer itself.

14
00:00:50.598 --> 00:00:58.721
And the third group, under Material 3, holds a ready-made catalog: all eighteen Basic Catalog components, already implemented in Material.

15
00:00:58.721 --> 00:01:04.371
Now the first trap, and it is the most expensive one, because it stops people before they start.

16
00:01:04.371 --> 00:01:08.668
The androidx dot a 2 u i group index lists only the model and the engine.

17
00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:10.846
Neither carries a Compose dependency.

18
00:01:10.846 --> 00:01:15.261
Read only that page and you will conclude Android has no renderer. It does.

19
00:01:15.261 --> 00:01:17.262
It is simply in a different group.

20
00:01:17.262 --> 00:01:18.459
So let us wire one up.

21
00:01:18.459 --> 00:01:22.216
Three things to get right in Gradle, and two of them are not obvious.

22
00:01:22.216 --> 00:01:24.946
Here is the dependency block from a working app.

23
00:01:24.946 --> 00:01:31.600
A Compose bill of materials, the usual Compose UI and Material 3, activity Compose, and then the five A2UI artifacts.

24
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.514
Everything resolves from Google's Maven repository, so make sure google is listed in your settings file.

25
00:01:37.514 --> 00:01:42.291
All five are pinned to the same alpha version, and you should keep them in lockstep.

26
00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:49.400
This is alpha software: the artifacts are released together and there is no compatibility promise across mismatched versions.

27
00:01:50.536 --> 00:01:54.092
Now the Android block, and two traps. First, the plugins.

28
00:01:54.092 --> 00:01:59.583
There are only two here: the Android application plugin and the Compose compiler plugin.

29
00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:08.380
Android Gradle Plugin nine rejects the old Kotlin Android plugin outright, with an error telling you it has not been required since AGP nine.

30
00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:14.869
If you copied a template from last year, delete that line, and delete the J V M toolchain block with it.

31
00:02:14.869 --> 00:02:16.865
Second, look at the compile SDK.

32
00:02:16.865 --> 00:02:24.165
Targeting thirty seven point one is not one property, it is two: compile SDK thirty seven, and compile SDK minor one.

33
00:02:24.165 --> 00:02:27.347
And use the J D K that ships inside Android Studio.

34
00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:31.028
Newer J D Ks break the build in ways that are hard to read.

35
00:02:31.028 --> 00:02:34.847
With the project building, the first object you construct is a catalog.

36
00:02:34.847 --> 00:02:38.398
It tells the renderer which components exist and how to draw them.

37
00:02:38.398 --> 00:02:41.507
If you use the Material catalog, you call this factory.

38
00:02:41.507 --> 00:02:46.653
Notice that it takes six parameters and none of them have defaults, which surprises people.

39
00:02:46.653 --> 00:02:51.346
The library deliberately ships no image loader, no video player and no audio player

40
00:02:51.346 --> 00:02:55.756
because those are opinionated dependencies and it will not choose one for you.

41
00:02:55.756 --> 00:03:00.563
You supply them, along with a URL opener, a message formatter, and a locale provider.

42
00:03:00.563 --> 00:03:07.743
If your payload never uses images, video or audio, no-op implementations are perfectly legitimate, which is what this app does.

43
00:03:08.958 --> 00:03:12.095
Here is how you write one, and here is the third trap.

44
00:03:12.095 --> 00:03:19.417
The concrete Material implementations of image, video and audio are marked internal, so you cannot reference or subclass them.

45
00:03:19.417 --> 00:03:21.741
Implement the public interfaces instead.

46
00:03:21.741 --> 00:03:27.436
And note the shape of the function: Typed Content is an extension function on A2UI component scope

47
00:03:27.436 --> 00:03:31.271
not a plain override, and its accessibility parameter is nullable.

48
00:03:31.271 --> 00:03:33.944
Getting that signature wrong by hand is fiddly

49
00:03:33.944 --> 00:03:38.128
so let the Kotlin compiler print its potential signatures for overriding

50
00:03:38.128 --> 00:03:39.697
and copy what it gives you.

51
00:03:40.438 --> 00:03:43.318
That is the setup done. The rendering itself is short.

52
00:03:43.318 --> 00:03:45.523
Six lines, and this is the whole of it.

53
00:03:45.523 --> 00:03:48.577
Create a message processor from your list of catalogs.

54
00:03:48.577 --> 00:03:53.667
Launch a coroutine that calls collect messages, which drains the processor's inbound queue

55
00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:56.834
and keep it alive for as long as the screen is composed.

56
00:03:56.834 --> 00:04:02.093
Forgetting that one line is a common mistake, and the symptom is a screen that never updates.

57
00:04:02.093 --> 00:04:03.676
Feed each parsed message in.

58
00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:08.314
Then collect active surfaces as Compose state, and hand a surface to A2UI Surface.

59
00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:12.385
From there the library owns the tree, the diffing and the recomposition.

60
00:04:13.438 --> 00:04:17.488
And that is enough to produce this: a brokerage positions screen, scrolling

61
00:04:17.488 --> 00:04:21.700
with every component driven by a payload rather than by layout code you wrote.

62
00:04:25.821 --> 00:04:30.520
Now the wire. Understanding three message types is most of understanding A2UI.

63
00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:34.122
Create surface opens a surface and names the catalog it will use.

64
00:04:34.122 --> 00:04:39.885
Update components sends components, and here is the important idea: it is an adjacency list, not a tree.

65
00:04:39.885 --> 00:04:44.374
Each component has an identifier and refers to its children by their identifiers.

66
00:04:44.374 --> 00:04:46.813
The component named root is the entry point.

67
00:04:46.813 --> 00:04:52.742
Because it is a flat list, you can send components before their children exist, and fill in the gaps later.

68
00:04:52.742 --> 00:04:56.068
Update data model sends the values those components bind to.

69
00:04:56.068 --> 00:05:03.660
Keep these separate in your head: structure and data travel independently, and that separation is what makes streaming and updates cheap.

70
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.147
Which brings us to how a component gets its value.

71
00:05:07.147 --> 00:05:12.500
Instead of a literal, a property can take a path: a JSON pointer into the surface's data model.

72
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.331
A text field pointing at slash amount displays whatever lives there.

73
00:05:16.331 --> 00:05:20.107
The part people miss is that on inputs this binding runs both ways.

74
00:05:20.107 --> 00:05:25.854
When the user types in that field or ticks that checkbox, the client data model is updated underneath.

75
00:05:25.854 --> 00:05:28.277
Nothing round trips to your agent for that.

76
00:05:28.277 --> 00:05:32.616
It is local, immediate, and it is why a form feels native rather than remote.

77
00:05:33.907 --> 00:05:37.748
Separating structure from data also buys you progressive rendering.

78
00:05:37.748 --> 00:05:39.066
Watch this form arrive.

79
00:05:39.066 --> 00:05:41.589
The agent sends it as four messages, not one

80
00:05:41.589 --> 00:05:46.290
so the client draws each piece as it lands: heading first, then the card of fields

81
00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:50.302
then the button, and finally the data model that fills every field in.

82
00:05:50.302 --> 00:05:53.570
The user sees structure immediately instead of a spinner.

83
00:05:56.282 --> 00:05:59.832
So far everything flows one way. Here is how it comes back.

84
00:05:59.832 --> 00:06:05.000
A button carries an action, and that action names an event plus the context to send with it.

85
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:11.291
When the user taps, the processor emits on outbound events, which is a Kotlin flow of client to server messages.

86
00:06:11.291 --> 00:06:18.930
This is the piece that is not in the documentation, so it is worth writing down: outbound events is the hook for everything a user does.

87
00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:22.919
Collect it, serialise the event, and send it wherever your agent lives.

88
00:06:22.919 --> 00:06:24.548
Here that is an H T T P post.

89
00:06:24.548 --> 00:06:29.828
The agent's reply streams straight back into the same processor, and the next surface renders.

90
00:06:31.192 --> 00:06:35.214
Here it is end to end. Ticking the box writes to the client data model.

91
00:06:35.214 --> 00:06:38.727
Tapping review sends the event, with that data model attached.

92
00:06:38.727 --> 00:06:43.542
The agent fills a template with the values the client just sent, and streams it back.

93
00:06:43.542 --> 00:06:49.208
Look at the frequency row: it reads Monthly, because the checkbox made the trip and came home again.

94
00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:52.551
Two surfaces, one round trip, no layout code on the client.

95
00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:56.166
Before you build anything, here is the whole list in one place.

96
00:06:56.166 --> 00:06:59.483
The build traps we covered. The catalog traps we covered.

97
00:06:59.483 --> 00:07:03.790
The four at the bottom are the dangerous ones, because none of them throw.

98
00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:07.748
They render the wrong screen and leave you looking at your own code.

99
00:07:07.748 --> 00:07:11.065
Update data model takes a key called value, not contents.

100
00:07:11.065 --> 00:07:15.081
Get that wrong and the form renders perfectly with every field empty.

101
00:07:15.081 --> 00:07:19.621
The variant enumerations are small and strict: text variants are h1 through h5

102
00:07:19.621 --> 00:07:20.552
caption and body

103
00:07:20.552 --> 00:07:25.674
so body medium is not valid and it will draw a red error chip where your text should be.

104
00:07:25.674 --> 00:07:28.700
Button variants are default, primary and borderless.

105
00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:33.531
And icon names are camel case, so check works and check underscore circle does not.

106
00:07:33.531 --> 00:07:39.002
When a component renders as an error chip, suspect an enum value before you suspect your code.

107
00:07:41.606 --> 00:07:45.155
One last thing, and for most teams it is the most important part.

108
00:07:45.155 --> 00:07:47.704
The renderer does not know what Material is.

109
00:07:47.704 --> 00:07:51.064
These are the two interfaces the whole system is built on.

110
00:07:51.064 --> 00:07:56.568
A component has a name, a description, a list of properties, and a composable content function.

111
00:07:56.568 --> 00:08:02.535
A catalog has an identifier, a collection of components, a collection of functions, and a theme schema.

112
00:08:02.535 --> 00:08:07.112
Material 3 dash a 2 u i is simply one implementation of that catalog interface.

113
00:08:07.112 --> 00:08:09.082
If you have your own design system

114
00:08:09.082 --> 00:08:13.601
you implement the same two interfaces and register yours instead, or alongside

115
00:08:13.601 --> 00:08:16.092
since a processor accepts several catalogs.

116
00:08:16.092 --> 00:08:21.885
You keep the protocol engine and supply the pixels, which is the right split for most product teams.

117
00:08:21.885 --> 00:08:22.890
That is the guide.

118
00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:28.362
The Android documentation page is the official reference, though it carries no code samples today.

119
00:08:28.362 --> 00:08:31.154
The source is browsable on cs dot android dot com.

120
00:08:31.154 --> 00:08:33.444
And if you are building your own renderer

121
00:08:33.444 --> 00:08:39.977
the conformance suites in the A2UI repository are thirty nine language-agnostic test definitions of correct behaviour

122
00:08:39.977 --> 00:08:44.053
which is the closest thing to a specification of what a renderer must do.

123
00:08:44.053 --> 00:08:46.845
Both projects shown here are on disk and runnable.

124
00:08:46.845 --> 00:08:51.759
Start with the static one, then move to the agent round trip once rendering makes sense.
